`,s.jsx(B,{items:[{q:"Prove K-means terminates, in three steps.",a:"First, neither step can increase WCSS: assignment only moves a point to a strictly closer centroid, and the mean is provably the minimiser of squared distance within a set. Second, there are at most K^n possible assignments — finite. Third, a non-increasing quantity over finitely many states cannot cycle forever, so it must halt."},{q:"K-means always converges. Why is that weaker than it sounds?",a:"Convergence is to a LOCAL minimum of WCSS, not the global one — the optimal partitioning problem is NP-hard. Two runs from different seeds can land on genuinely different clusterings with different costs, which is why libraries run it n_init times and keep the best."},{q:"Explain the exact failure that K-means++ prevents.",a:"Uniform random initialisation can drop two centroids inside the same dense blob while a real, well-separated cluster gets none. The algorithm then splits one true cluster in half and merges two others, and no amount of iteration escapes it. D^2 sampling makes far-from-everything points overwhelmingly likely to be chosen next."},{q:"First centroid is at (2,2); candidates sit at (2,3) and at (8,8), (9,7), (8,9). Roughly where does the second centroid land, and why?",a:"Almost certainly in the far group. Squared distances are 1 versus 72, 74, and 85, so the near candidate gets under half a percent of the probability mass and the distant cluster gets over 99%. That is precisely the spreading behaviour K-means++ is designed for."},{q:"What is the per-iteration cost, and which factor should worry you first?",a:"O(n*K*p) per iteration, with typically 10 to 100 iterations. n scales fine because the cost is linear in it; the factor to watch is p, since high dimensionality both increases the per-distance cost and degrades the meaningfulness of Euclidean distance itself."}]}),`
